  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/aa62dda1abb7bc6bca1b2e6cdf356b52946820912ec6318e5901273a546ee96a.png)\n\nConsider the square region $\u0394$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\u0394={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the bottom side of $\u0394$ under the action of $T?$",
  "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $\u0394$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png)",
